          %p (work in progress)
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Differential linear logic is an extension of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/co-Kleisli+category"} co-Kleisli category
            are differentiable, and it was then reflected in the syntax by these new inference rules. Thus, one really needs to take the point of view of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computational+trinitarianism"} computational trinitarianism
            in order to understand the transition from linear logic to differential linear logic. It is more difficult to understand naively the proofs of differential linear logic than the ones of linear logic.
          %p Differential linear logic can be presented in different guises whether we include the additives or not, and whether we include the promotion or not. We start by presenting the version without additives and with promotion. We will refer to this logic as DiLL.
          %h2#syntax Syntax
          %p
            There are two ways to present the syntax in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequent+calculus"} sequent calculus
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof+nets"} proof nets
            and to divide by two the number of inference rules. Using the monolateral version (or proof nets) require to quotient the formulas by some equalities related to negation and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/De+Morgan+duality"} De Morgan duality
          %h3#formulas__generalities Formulas - Generalities
          %p There are no novelties concerning formulas compared to linear logic. We will work with the formulas of Multiplicative Exponential Linear Logic (MELL). In the next subsection, the difference between MELL and DiLL will appear as additional inference rules.
          %p Formulas are given by induction starting from atoms and applying connectives. In accordance with the tradition, formulas and atoms are denoted in the same way by upper case letters:
          %ul
            %li
              Atoms are denoted by upper case letter
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                      %mo ,
                      %mo .
                      %mo .
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B,...
            %li
              If we have two formulas
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \otimes B
            %li
              If we have two formulas
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⅋
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \parr B
            %li
              If we have a formula
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo !
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} !{A}
            %li
              If we have a formula
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ?
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ?{A}
            %li
              If we have a formula
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi A
                        %mo ⊥
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^\perp
            %li
              We have the formula
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            %li
              We have the formula
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊥
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot
          %h3#formulas__quotienting_by_duality_in_monolateral_syntax Formulas - Quotienting by duality in monolateral syntax
          %p If we want to use the monolateral sequent calculus (or proof nets), formulas must be quotiented by the following equalities:
          %ul
            %li
              If we have a formula
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi A
                        %mrow
                          %mo ⊥
                          %mo ⊥
                      %mo =
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^{\perp\perp} = A
            %li
              If we have two formulas
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi B
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ⊥
                      %mo =
                      %msup
                        %mi A
                        %mo ⊥
                      %mo ⅋
                      %msup
                        %mi B
                        %mo ⊥
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A \otimes B)^\perp = A^\perp \parr B^\perp
            %li
              If we have two formulas
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⅋
                      %mi B
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ⊥
                      %mo =
                      %msup
                        %mi A
                        %mo ⊥
                      %mo ⊗
                      %msup
                        %mi B
                        %mo ⊥
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A \parr B)^\perp = A^\perp \otimes B^\perp
            %li
              If we have a formula
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
