          %h3#inference_rules__monolateral_syntax Inference rules - Monolateral syntax
          %h2#isomorphisms_in_bilateral_syntax Isomorphisms in bilateral syntax
          %h2#cut_elimination Cut elimination
          %p
            There is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rewriting+system"} rewriting system
            which, given an instance of the cut rule in a proof, reduces the proof by making the cut go higher in the proof. There are key (principal) cases which induce important commuting diagrams in the categorical semantics and there are commuting cases which are less important. The idea is that when a cut arrives at the beginning of the proof, i.e. at the level of the leaves which are axioms, we can finally eliminate it by a last rewriting step. This is, when the cut is made against an axiom rule, that we can eliminate it. We know that this rewriting system is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strongly+normalizing"} strongly normalizing
            and that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normal+forms"} normal forms
            are the cut-free proofs. It means that by using rewriting rules in the order you want, at the end you obtain a proof without cut, thus the name
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cut-elimination"} cut-elimination
          %h3#cut_elimination_steps Cut elimination steps
          %h3#what_is_an_isomorphism What is an isomorphism?
          %h2#semantics Semantics
          %h3#categorical_semantics Categorical semantics
          %p
            We need a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%2A-autonomous+category"} *-autonomous category
            in order to interpret the connectives and rules related to these connectives:
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⅋
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊥
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mrow
                      %mo ⊥
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes, \parr, 1, \bot, {}^{\perp}
          %p This category must be enriched over commutative monoids in order to be able to interpret the sum of proofs and null proofs.
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              We say that a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%2A-autonomous+category"} *-autonomous category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              is enriched over commutative monoids if it is enriched over commutative monoids as a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
              i.e. every hom-set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}[A,B]
              is a commutative monoid and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -\otimes-
              as well as
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ;
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -;-
              are bilinear over morphisms i.e. preserve addition and zero in every variable.
          %p
            The tricky part is to interpret the exponentials
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo !
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ?
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} !,?
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo !
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} !
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ?
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ?
            will follow by the
            = succeed "-autonomous" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
          %ul
            %li
              A natural transformation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo !
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo !
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mo !
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} !A \otimes !A \rightarrow !A
              to interpret the cocontraction.
            %li
              A natural transformation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo !
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mo !
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo !
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} !A \rightarrow !A \otimes !A
              to interpret the contraction.
            %li
              A natural transformation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                    %mo →
                    %mo !
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I \rightarrow !A
              to interpret the coweakening.
            %li
              A natural transformation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo !
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} !A \rightarrow I
              to interpret the weakening.
            %li
              A natural transformation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mo !
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \rightarrow !A
              to interpret the codereliction.
            %li
              A natural transformation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo !
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} !A \rightarrow A
              to interpret the dereliction.
          %p
            Thus, for every object
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo !
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} !A
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bimonoid"} bimonoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} !
            will be a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comonad"} comonad
          %p (…)
          %h3#concrete_models Concrete models
          %p (…)
          %h2#the_logic_at_work The logic at work
          %h3#the_cokleisli_category The co-Kleisli category
          %h3#differentiating_a_proof Differentiating a proof
          %p
            We start from a proof of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo !
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} !A \vdash B
            and by using as only exponential rules cocontraction and codereliction, we obtain a proof of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo !
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊸
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} !A \vdash A \multimap B
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:A \rightarrow B
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi df
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊸
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} df: A \rightarrow (A \multimap B)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            which associates to every point of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            the linear approximation of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            around this point.
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mfrac
                      %mfrac
                        %mrow
                          %mfrac
                            %mrow
                              %msup
                                %mfrac
                                  %mrow
                                  %mrow
                                    %mo !
                                    %mi A
                                    %mo ⊢
                                    %mo !
                                    %mi A
                                %mi ax
                              %mfrac
                                %mrow
                                  %msup
                                    %mfrac
                                      %mrow
                                      %mrow
                                        %mi A
                                        %mo ⊢
                                        %mi A
                                    %mi ax
                                %mrow
                                  %mi A
                                  %mo ⊢
                                  %mo !
                                  %mi A
                            %mrow
                              %mo !
                              %mi A
                              %mo ,
                              %mi A
                              %mo ⊢
                              %mo !
                              %mi A
                          %mo !
                          %mi A
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi B
                        %mrow
                          %mo !
                          %mi A
                          %mo ,
                          %mi A
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi B
                      %mrow
                        %mo !
                        %mi A
                        %mo ⊢
                        %msup
                          %mi A
                          %mo ⊥
                        %mo ,
                        %mi B
                    %mrow
                      %mo !
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊸
                      %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{\frac{\frac{\frac{\frac{}{!A \vdash !A}^{ax} \frac{\frac{}{A \vdash A}^{ax}}{A \vdash !A}}{!A,A \vdash !A} !A \vdash B}{!A,A \vdash B}}{!A \vdash A^{\perp},B}}{!A \vdash A \multimap B}
